  "worked_solution": "To solve this problem, we use the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series.\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}=\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d)\n$$\n\nSince we know the first two terms of the arithmetic series, we can find its common difference, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{2}-{a}_{1}=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting ${a}_{1}=-3,$ $d=3,$ and ${S}_{N}=27$ into the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{N} & =\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d) \\\\ 27 & =\\frac{N}{2}(2(-3)+(N-1)(3)) \\\\ 54 & =N(-6+3N-3) \\\\ 54 & =N(3N-9) \\\\ 54 & =3{N}^{2}-9N\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the above equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{N}^{2}-9N-54 & =0 \\\\ {N}^{2}-3N-18 & =0 \\\\ (N-6)(N+3) & =0 \\\\ N & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we take the positive solution because $N$ can't be negative.",
